For this assessment, I recommend building it in two major stages: Backend first, then Frontend. Don't start with the map UI. First make the Go APIs, database, geofence detection, and WebSocket alerts work independently. Once the backend is stable, connect React to it.

Since this is a full-stack assessment, the goal should be more than making the demo work: you should be able to explain the architecture, data flow, geospatial logic, and design decisions during the 30–45 minute video.
1. Recommended technology stack
Backend first
Go + PostgreSQL + PostGIS

REST APIs, geofence detection, vehicle tracking, alert processing


Real-time communication

Gorilla WebSocket for broadcasting entry/exit alerts


Frontend — second stage

React + TypeScript + Vite + Tailwind CSS + React Leaflet


Infrastructure

Docker, Docker Compose, GitHub, Docker Hub, Render/Railway or another Docker-compatible host, Vercel


Layer	Technology	Purpose
HTTP server	Go net/http or Chi	REST endpoints
Database	PostgreSQL	Persistent data
Geospatial	PostGIS	Point-in-polygon queries
Database access	pgx + sqlc or pgx directly	Queries and persistence
WebSocket	Gorilla WebSocket	Live alerts
Validation	Go validation / custom validation	Request validation
Logging	slog	Structured logs
Frontend	React + TypeScript	Dashboard
Maps	Leaflet + React Leaflet	Geofence polygons and vehicle markers
API state	TanStack Query	Fetching, caching, mutations

Why PostGIS? Geofencing is fundamentally a geospatial problem. PostGIS provides spatial functions and indexing, so you can explain how the system would scale beyond a simple in-memory implementation.

2. High-level system architecture
This is the architecture I would implement.
