Filter and inspect past events




Frontend implementation order
1. Set up Vite, React, TypeScript and Tailwind.
2. Create application layout and navigation.
3. Integrate geofence listing and creation.
4. Integrate vehicle registration and listing.
5. Integrate location updates.
6. Add Leaflet map with polygons and markers.
7. Add alert configuration.
8. Connect WebSocket and implement live notifications.
9. Add violation history with filters and pagination.
10. Add loading, empty, error and responsive states.
I recommend building the map after basic CRUD screens. That way, you can validate the backend without debugging map interactions at the same time.

9. Testing strategy
Before moving from backend to frontend, test the most important scenario end-to-end.

Use this example:

Create a geofence
{
  "name": "Restricted Zone",
  "description": "Test restricted area",
  "coordinates": [
    [12.9710, 77.5900],
    [12.9810, 77.5900],
    [12.9810, 77.6000],
    [12.9710, 77.6000],
    [12.9710, 77.5900]
  ],
  "category": "restricted_zone"
}


Register a vehicle
{
  "vehicle_number": "KA-01-AB-1234",
  "driver_name": "John Doe",
  "vehicle_type": "truck",
  "phone": "+919876543210"
}


Configure an alert
{
  "geofence_id": "geo_123",
  "vehicle_id": "veh_456",
  "event_type": "both"
}


Send a location outside the geofence
{
  "vehicle_id": "veh_456",
  "latitude": 12.9650,
  "longitude": 77.5850,
  "timestamp": "2026-11-07T10:00:00Z"
}


Send a location inside the geofence
{
  "vehicle_id": "veh_456",
  "latitude": 12.9750,
  "longitude": 77.5950,
  "timestamp": "2026-11-07T10:01:00Z"
}


Expected behavior:
- The first update should not generate an entry event.
- The second update should generate exactly one entry event.
- A connected WebSocket client should receive the alert.
- Violation history should contain that event.
- The current location endpoint should show the vehicle inside the restricted zone.
Then send another location outside the zone and verify that an exit event is generated.

Also test duplicate location updates, overlapping polygons, boundary points, unknown vehicle IDs, invalid polygons, and alert rules for all vehicles.

10. Docker and deployment
Your root docker-compose.yml should initially run:
docker-compose
    |
    |-- backend (Go API)
    |
    |-- postgres (PostgreSQL + PostGIS)


Later, add the frontend if you want to run the entire application locally through Docker.

Example:
services:
  postgres:
    image: postgis/postgis:16-3.4
    environment:
      POSTGRES_USER: geouser
      POSTGRES_PASSWORD: geopassword
      POSTGRES_DB: geofencing
    ports:
      - "5432:5432"
    volumes:
      - postgres_data:/var/lib/postgresql/data

  backend:
    build: ./backend
    ports:
      - "8080:8080"
    env_file:
      - ./backend/.env
    depends_on:
      - postgres

volumes:
  postgres_data:


For production, add database health checks, persistent secrets, migrations, and proper service readiness handling. Don't use the example database password in a public deployment.

Deployment sequence:
1. Push backend source to a private GitHub repository.
2. Build and push backend image to Docker Hub.
3. Deploy backend and PostgreSQL/PostGIS.
4. Configure production environment variables.
5. Deploy React frontend to Vercel.
6. Configure frontend API and WebSocket URLs.
7. Test the deployed application end-to-end.
11. Common mistakes to avoid
These are particularly important for this assessment:
Mistake	Better approach
Checking only the current location	Compare previous and current geofence membership
Sending alerts for every location update	Send only on entry/exit transitions
Broadcasting before database commit	Persist first, broadcast after commit
Saving only current location	Maintain location history
Ignoring overlapping geofences	Check all matching geofences
Using latitude/longitude in the wrong order	Convert API coordinates correctly
Treating a missing vehicle ID as a global alert rule	Use NULL for all-vehicle rules
Returning different response formats	Follow the specified JSON shapes consistently
Forgetting time_ns on error responses	Include timing on every HTTP response
Broadcasting synchronously to every socket	Use a buffered event channel and connection hub

One more important detail: WebSocket clients can disconnect or be slow. A slow client must not block all other clients. Give each connection its own buffered send channel and handle failed writes by disconnecting that client.
12. Suggested project timeline
For a focused implementation, I'd plan approximately 10–14 working days. This assumes you can spend a few hours each day; allow more time if Go and PostGIS are new to you.
Days	Focus	Milestone
1–2	Go setup, database, migrations, geofence CRUD	Geofence APIs
3	Vehicle CRUD	Vehicle APIs
4–5	Location tracking and geospatial detection	Entry/exit logic
6	Alert rules and WebSocket	Live alerts
7	Violation history, testing, error handling	Complete backend
8	React setup and dashboard	Frontend foundation
9–10	CRUD screens and map	Map integration
11	WebSocket and live notifications	Real-time UI
12	History, filters and polish	Complete frontend
13–14	Docker, deployment, documentation and video	Submission

13. Final submission checklist
- [ ] All nine REST endpoints match the required paths and methods.
- [ ] Every HTTP response includes time_ns.
- [ ] Point-in-polygon detection works.
- [ ] Entry and exit events are detected correctly.
- [ ] Alert configurations support vehicle-specific and all-vehicle rules.
- [ ] WebSocket supports multiple clients.
- [ ] Historical events are stored and filterable.
- [ ] React dashboard integrates with all APIs.
- [ ] Map displays geofences and vehicles.
- [ ] Docker Compose starts the local system.
- [ ] Backend Docker image is pushed to Docker Hub.
- [ ] Backend and frontend are deployed.
- [ ] SETUP.md contains setup and testing instructions.
- [ ] Private GitHub repository has all requested collaborators.
- [ ] 30–45 minute technical walkthrough is recorded.
- [ ] Assessment Google Form is submitted.

My recommendation: start with the backend foundation
Don't try to write all the code at once. Build each module, test it, and move to the next.

Your first coding milestone should be:
1. Create the GitHub repository.
2. Create the backend folder.
3. Initialize the Go module.
4. Set up PostgreSQL/PostGIS with Docker Compose.
5. Connect Go to PostgreSQL.
6. Create the first database migration for geofences.
Once that works, start POST /geofences and GET /geofences.

This will give you a solid foundation before tackling the more challenging location and real-time logic.

I can guide you through implementing this project step by step, starting with the Go backend setup and database connection.